#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# discriminant

#% text
# `discriminant(p, x)` gives the discriminant of the polynomial `p` with respect to `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The discriminant is `a^(2n - 2)` times the product of the squared differences of the roots, `a` the leading coefficient and `n` the degree. It is 0 exactly when `p` has a repeated root.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
discriminant(a*x^2 + b*x + c, x)
#% output
#= -4*a*c + b^2

#% cell
discriminant(x^3 + p*x + q, x)
#% output
#= -27*q^2 - 4*p^3

#% cell
discriminant(x^2 - 2*x + 1, x)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# For which `c` the cubic `x^3 - 3x + c` has a double root:

#% cell
solve(discriminant(x^3 - 3*x + c, x) == 0, c)
#% output
#= [[c -> -2], [c -> 2]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The sign of the discriminant tells a cubic's real roots: positive for three, negative for one:

#% cell
[discriminant(x^3 - 3*x + 1, x), discriminant(x^3 + x + 1, x)]
#% output
#= [81, -31]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `resultant`, `roots`, `factor_square_free`
